         <ab>
            
            
            <persName type="reg">
               <forename>Paul</forename> <surname>Kieffer</surname>
            </persName>
            <persName type="full">Paul Kieffer</persName>
            <date type="birth" when="1881-07-16"/>
            <date type="death" when="1969-06-08"/>
            <note type="gender">M</note>
         </ab>
         <div type="bio">
            <p>Rhodes Scholar, Harvard graduate, and partner in the law firm Quinn, Keiffer and Woodward. Kieffer was the uncle of <name type="person" key="ID_MISSING">Helen Kieffer</name> (1905-1958), the young woman who became <name type="person" key="joyc07">Lucia Joyce</name>’s friend and dance companion in Paris and Deauville in 1922 and 1923. Though he was the successor of <name type="person" key="quin70">John Quinn</name>, who had defended <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">The Little Review</name>’s publication of <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Ulysses</name>, when asked by JJ and <name type="person" key="beac87">Sylvia Beach</name> to represent JJ in legal action against <name type="person" key="roth93">Samuel Roth</name>’s unauthorized publication of <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Ulysses</name> in 1926, Kieffer cabled a curt refusal: “Regret unable to take case.” <hi rend="bold">William Brockman</hi>
                </p>
         </div>
      </body>
